2186088225 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=5.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088225 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a70227b-6237-55b5-b378-b7662770487e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088225 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\pm 3\\text{i},$ and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+3x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ {y}^{″} & =6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ -6\\alpha =-12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3P\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+x(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3Q+2=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P=2,Q=1.$\n\nSo, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088394 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088394 #edb/ref 1069 "q-8114" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088394 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088394 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,\\,y(0)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088394 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f684abde-2923-5a1b-8719-a76da5c1d85c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088394 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda +1)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+2(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(-4\\alpha -3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\ -4\\alpha -3\\beta =-25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =4$ and $\\beta =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4 & =4 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-x}-Bx{e}^{-x}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-A+B+6 & =0 \\\\ B & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=0,B=-6.$\n\nSo, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-6x{e}^{-x}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088653 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088653 #edb/ref 1061 24264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088653 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "91f805b4-d536-567b-92f7-524437b74146" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088653 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088654 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088654 #edb/ref 1061 24265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088654 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fcefea54-b58a-521e-b82e-14299e2f316d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088654 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088655 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088655 #edb/ref 1061 24266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088655 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "38062346-4103-5ff0-815d-26c1948b8d40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088655 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088656 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088656 #edb/ref 1061 24267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088656 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ecd022a7-62c1-51a9-8e53-2f732b860fdf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088656 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088661 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088661 #edb/ref 1061 25051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088661 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0a04129d-2ef1-51c2-a987-dc643edb1e96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088661 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088663 #edb/ref 1061 25098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088663 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "289c0c9c-bc55-5a0e-a317-ab36694e1a6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088663 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088950 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that second-order inhomogeneous differential equation takes the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $a(x),$ $b(x),$ and $c(x)$ are functions of $x$ only and $f(x)$ is a nonzero function.\n\nIn general, we say that a second-order ODE has **sinusoidal forcing** if $f(x)$ is a sinusoidal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = a \\cos \\omega x + b \\sin \\omega x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\omega$ is a constant.\n\nFor example, the ODE below has sinusoidal forcing:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y= 2 \\cos 2 x\n$$\n\nSolving a second-order ODE with sinusoidal forcing is similar to the method for solving a second-order ODE with polynomial forcing. The general solution is still the sum of the complementary solution $y_c$ (i.e., the solution to the associated homogeneous ODE) and a particular solution $y_p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p\n$$\n\nThe only difference is in how we find a particular solution. Given a second-order ODE with sinusoidal forcing $f(x) = a \\cos \\omega x + b \\sin \\omega x,$ provided that $\\sin \\omega x$ and $\\cos \\omega x$ do not feature in the complementary solution, then we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha \\cos \\omega x + \\beta  \\sin \\omega x.\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are unknown constants to be determined." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088950 #edb/ref 1065 4066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088950 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c922743e-1a32-51dd-a109-feac1b1930c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088950 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088951 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a second-order ODE with sinusoidal forcing $f(x) = a \\cos \\omega x + b \\sin \\omega x,$ if $\\cos \\omega x$ or $\\sin \\omega x$ *is* a term in the complementary solution, then $a \\cos \\omega x + b \\sin \\omega x$ *cannot* be a particular solution. Instead, we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = x(\\alpha \\cos \\omega x + \\beta  \\sin \\omega x).\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the solution to the following ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + y= 4 \\cos x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\text{i},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = \\textrm{i}$ or $\\lambda =  -\\textrm{i}.$ Consequently, the complimentary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\cos x + B \\sin x.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $4 \\cos x$ and $\\cos x$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = x (\\alpha \\cos x + \\beta \\sin x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x), \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -2\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = 0$ and $\\beta = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = 2x \\sin{x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088951 #edb/ref 1065 4067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088951 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "703ca0f2-aa95-5326-811b-83d5a458ccd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088951 #edb/ref 1067 "The Case When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution Have the Same Term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088955 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's solve the following second-order ODE with sinusoidal forcing:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y= 2 \\cos 2 x\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation (or auxiliary equation) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = 1$ or $\\lambda = -2.$ Consequently, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $2 \\cos 2x,$ and $\\cos 2x$ does not appear as a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha \\cos 2x + \\beta  \\sin 2x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ (2\\beta -6\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(-6\\beta -2\\alpha )\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding terms on both sides should have the same coefficients. Equating the coefficients of like terms, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta -6\\alpha =2 \\\\ -6\\beta -2\\alpha =0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, it follows that \n\n$$\n- 6 \\beta - 2 \\alpha = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\alpha = - 3 \\beta.\n$$\n\n Substituting $\\alpha=-3\\beta$ into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\beta -6(-3)\\beta  & =2 \\\\ 20\\beta  & =2 \\\\ \\beta  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting into $\\alpha = -3\\beta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\alpha = -3(0.1) = -0.3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = - 0.3 \\cos 2x + 0.1  \\sin 2x,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}-0.3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+0.1\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088955 #edb/ref 1065 4139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088955 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9ba5d11b-8158-56a5-9e90-b52297ff5938" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088955 #edb/ref 1067 "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Sinusoidal Forcing" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333293 #edb/ref 1080 "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333293 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333293 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21eafcc3-ef61-5480-b57c-348255ceb796" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333663 #edb/ref 1080 "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333663 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333663 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "49290232-5081-5b5d-bd61-6db73b6ba662" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334360 #edb/ref 1080 "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334360 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334360 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9b48e57b-80c2-5c26-8e33-43322de288da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334377 #edb/ref 1080 "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334377 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186